1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,923
Sam's first batch happened on a Wednesday afternoon with no checklist and two

2
00:00:04,923 --> 00:00:06,348
mistakes to show for it.

3
00:00:06,348 --> 00:00:11,217
Yours happens this afternoon with the checklist already built: same twenty rows, a

4
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:13,533
five-lecture head start he didn't have.

5
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:15,908
Pull up your angle map from Section 2.6.

6
00:00:15,908 --> 00:00:17,631
Every row becomes one prompt.

7
00:00:17,631 --> 00:00:22,441
Unlike Harlan's first pass, you're building the checks in from the start, because

8
00:00:22,441 --> 00:00:25,707
you've already seen where a first batch usually breaks.

9
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:30,576
Why did Harlan's first pass skip straight to generating instead of checking first?

10
00:00:30,576 --> 00:00:33,545
Because nobody had written down what to check yet.

11
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:37,583
You have, which is the entire advantage this workshop is banking on.

12
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:41,800
This is the one lecture in this section that isn't teaching a new idea.

13
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:46,491
It's the pause where you actually do the thing, on your own product, instead of

14
00:00:46,491 --> 00:00:48,391
watching Harlan's account do it.

15
00:00:48,391 --> 00:00:51,776
Close anything else you were half-doing before you start.

16
00:00:51,776 --> 00:00:56,586
An hour of focused attention here saves you from the exact two mistakes that cost

17
00:00:56,586 --> 00:00:58,130
Sam a Wednesday afternoon.

18
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:03,893
This is the actual template, and every line traces back to a specific lecture you've

19
00:01:03,893 --> 00:01:05,134
already sat through.

20
00:01:05,134 --> 00:01:08,175
Subject and scene come from the angle map itself.

21
00:01:08,175 --> 00:01:10,036
The brand lock is Section 3.3.

22
00:01:10,036 --> 00:01:11,836
The text rule is Section 3.4.

23
00:01:11,836 --> 00:01:15,187
The face rule is Section 3.3 again, applied per angle.

24
00:01:15,187 --> 00:01:18,290
And the packshot-or-lifestyle call is Section 3.5.

25
00:01:18,290 --> 00:01:23,130
Run all twenty rows through these five questions, and you're not guessing at a

26
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:26,543
prompt, you're filling out a checklist you already own.

27
00:01:26,543 --> 00:01:30,205
Save this template somewhere you'll actually find it again.

28
00:01:30,205 --> 00:01:34,921
The next product you brief, and the one after that, start from the same five

29
00:01:34,921 --> 00:01:40,010
questions, not from a blank page and a hope that you remember what went wrong last

30
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:40,320
time.

31
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:44,788
Notice what this template actually is, underneath the checklist framing.

32
00:01:44,788 --> 00:01:49,690
It's every mistake this section named, turned into a question you answer before

33
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:52,917
generating rather than a problem you discover after.

34
00:01:52,917 --> 00:01:58,006
That's the entire shape of what "the machine" means in this course: not a piece of

35
00:01:58,006 --> 00:02:03,219
software, a short list of questions that catches a known failure before it costs you

36
00:02:03,219 --> 00:02:03,777
anything.

37
00:02:04,327 --> 00:02:08,809
About an hour, total, and notice most of it isn't generation time at all.

38
00:02:08,809 --> 00:02:13,720
It's the checking and fixing this section spent five lectures teaching you to do

39
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:14,272
properly.

40
00:02:14,272 --> 00:02:19,245
If your first batch comes back with real problems, seven or eight statics needing

41
00:02:19,245 --> 00:02:21,577
fixes, don't read that as a bad batch.

42
00:02:21,577 --> 00:02:22,621
Harlan's did too.

43
00:02:22,621 --> 00:02:27,655
Read it as confirmation that the checks in this workshop are catching exactly what

44
00:02:27,655 --> 00:02:29,251
they're supposed to catch.

45
00:02:29,251 --> 00:02:34,162
Notice, too, that the regenerate step only touches the flagged statics, the same

46
00:02:34,162 --> 00:02:35,881
discipline from Section 3.3.

47
00:02:35,881 --> 00:02:40,915
If you find yourself regenerating all twenty because a few came back wrong, you've

48
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:44,966
slipped back into the habit this whole section was built to break.

49
00:02:44,966 --> 00:02:49,755
An hour sounds like a lot for twenty images, until you compare it to what it's

50
00:02:49,755 --> 00:02:50,369
replacing.

51
00:02:50,369 --> 00:02:55,341
A shoot covering the same ground is days of scheduling before a single frame gets

52
00:02:55,341 --> 00:02:55,648
shot.

53
00:02:55,648 --> 00:03:00,498
This hour is the entire production timeline, checks included, and most of it is

54
00:03:00,498 --> 00:03:02,278
thinking rather than waiting.

55
00:03:02,828 --> 00:03:07,303
Both of these mistakes are the exact ones Harlan's first batch already made.

56
00:03:07,303 --> 00:03:11,896
Skip the reference image and describe a colour in words, and you'll get colour

57
00:03:11,896 --> 00:03:15,076
drift, for the same reason it happened in Section 3.2.

58
00:03:15,076 --> 00:03:19,964
Bake in an exact claim because the design tool feels like an extra step, and you'll

59
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:22,967
get the same garbled text on the same kind of line.

60
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:25,735
You've already watched both of these fail once.

61
00:03:25,735 --> 00:03:30,563
That's the actual value of doing this workshop after the section instead of before

62
00:03:30,563 --> 00:03:30,740
it.

63
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:35,627
If this were the first lecture in Section three rather than the last, both mistakes

64
00:03:35,627 --> 00:03:40,515
would feel like reasonable shortcuts instead of familiar traps, and you'd learn the

65
00:03:40,515 --> 00:03:45,049
same lesson Harlan did, the expensive way, on your own batch instead of hers.

66
00:03:45,049 --> 00:03:49,878
Watch for the moment it feels tempting to skip a step because "this one's probably

67
00:03:49,878 --> 00:03:54,059
fine." That feeling is exactly what preceded both of Harlan's failures.

68
00:03:54,059 --> 00:03:57,062
Neither felt like a real risk in the moment either.

69
00:03:57,062 --> 00:04:01,949
The whole value of a checklist is that it doesn't care how confident you feel about

70
00:04:01,949 --> 00:04:02,656
skipping it.

71
00:04:03,206 --> 00:04:06,060
One honest expectation to set before you start.

72
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:11,040
Even with every check built in from the start, some statics will still need a fix.

73
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:13,044
That's not this workshop failing.

74
00:04:13,044 --> 00:04:18,084
It's the same shape of result Harlan got, and the actual measure of success isn't a

75
00:04:18,084 --> 00:04:22,881
perfect first pass, it's whether every problem gets caught here, before Section

76
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:25,917
seven's test spends real money finding it instead.

77
00:04:25,917 --> 00:04:30,897
Measure yourself against that standard when you finish, not against a tidy number.

78
00:04:30,897 --> 00:04:34,541
Twenty for twenty on the first try would be a nice surprise.

79
00:04:34,541 --> 00:04:39,520
Twenty for twenty after one careful regeneration pass is the actual goal, and it's

80
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:43,589
the same result either way by the time this batch is ready to test.

81
00:04:43,589 --> 00:04:48,629
If your numbers come back worse than Harlan's, thirteen or fourteen correct instead

82
00:04:48,629 --> 00:04:53,730
of a comfortable majority, that's worth a second look at the angle map itself rather

83
00:04:53,730 --> 00:04:55,248
than the generation step.

84
00:04:55,248 --> 00:04:59,985
A batch that fails broadly usually traces back to a brief that wasn't specific

85
00:04:59,985 --> 00:05:02,778
enough, not to the generator having a bad day.

86
00:05:03,328 --> 00:05:06,292
Don't leave this session with a half-checked batch.

87
00:05:06,292 --> 00:05:10,999
The whole point of today was catching problems before they cost real test budget,

88
00:05:10,999 --> 00:05:15,765
and that only works if you actually finish the pass: generate, check colour, check

89
00:05:15,765 --> 00:05:17,624
text, regenerate what's flagged.

90
00:05:17,624 --> 00:05:22,448
A batch you meant to finish tomorrow is a batch that tests badly next week, because

91
00:05:22,448 --> 00:05:25,702
the discipline you skipped doesn't come back on its own.

92
00:05:25,702 --> 00:05:27,620
One lecture left in this section.

93
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:32,327
Then Section four takes everything you've just built and does it again for video,

94
00:05:32,327 --> 00:05:36,047
where a new set of failures, and a new set of fixes, is waiting.

95
00:05:36,047 --> 00:05:38,778
Bring B03 with you, finished, not half-checked.
